WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.107
- [Joanna] Laptops, water, devastation.

00:00:07.706 --> 00:00:10.456
(dramatic music)

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.900
Now, if you were the
owner of one of these,

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.450
you'd probably first cry

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:20.240
and then you'd immediately
go to the Apple store.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.340
And that's exactly what
Apple wants you to do.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.890
The company and many others

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.800
restricts how and where
you can repair your stuff

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.840
in a variety of ways.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.880
Apple says this is to make
sure customers receive

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.570
safe and reliable repair services.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.940
But now some elected
officials and lawmakers

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.740
wanna give you more choices,

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.990
what's generally called
the right to repair.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:43.010
This doesn't mean you have
to go all Rosie the Riveter

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.410
with your own tools.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.290
This is also about making
it much easier for others,

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:50.863
including independent repair
shops to help fix your stuff.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.350
That doesn't look good.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.820
- This doesn't look good
at all and it's corroded.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:57.060
The worst part is over here.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:57.893
- Oh boy.

00:00:57.893 --> 00:00:58.840
The Fair Repair Act,

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.090
which was introduced in Congress in June

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.000
would require original
equipment manufacturers

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.330
to make available certain documentation,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:10.170
diagnostic and repair information
to independent providers.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.650
Over 20 states have introduced
similarly worded bills,

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.313
none have been enacted yet.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.740
So what does this all mean,

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.330
and would this really help you,

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.640
the hopeless person with a broken gadget?

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.970
Yes, it probably would.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:28.020
Certainly my journey to
repair two very dead Macooks

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.683
showed it could save
you both time and money.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.960
Let's first talk about these two machines.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.070
This is a 13 inch 2017 MacBook Pro,

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:40.850
which at the time cost $1,400.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:44.713
The second, a 2020 MacBook
Air that cost $1,100.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:48.270
Both I grabbed from my IT department

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.480
and were marked as having water damage.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.350
I promise you I did not
actually spill water on these

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.470
to break them.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:57.550
So I packed them up and
went to the Apple store.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.080
Since they were out of warranty,

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.600
and I don't have AppleCare Plus,

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.770
which covers accidental damage.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:05.340
I walked out of the store, pretty sad,

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.270
799 to fix the Air,

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.810
999 to fix the Pro.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:13.340
And the genius I spoke
to basically suggested

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.570
I just buy some new machines instead.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:16.450
Shocker.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:17.790
Then Mike's Tech Shop,

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:21.270
an authorized Apple service
provider had even worse news.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.970
It sends the laptops
out to Apple to be fixed

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.317
and it adds its own labor charge,

00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:31.317
$870 for the Air, 1,170 for the Pro.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.510
Yeah, Nope.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.530
Plus both of those
stores said it would take

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.060
five to seven days for the repair.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.530
That's when I decided to go to a shop

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.960
that doesn't have any
relationship with Apple,

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:43.643
such as Rossman Repair.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.190
The owner, Louis Rossman
has been an advocate

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.420
about the right to repair.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.060
- The only option is to go to Apple

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.980
and to have this three or $4,000 device

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.790
fixed by them for 1500 dollars.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.070
- [Joanna] He's also got
a popular YouTube channel

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:00.000
where he often criticizes
Apple's repair restrictions,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.010
champions the right to repair

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.920
and shares his opinion on various topics.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.970
- It's really important
to me with right to repair

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:10.160
that what we're asking
for, be very reasonable.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.500
- [Joanna] His price
quote, $325 for the Pro,

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:15.990
but a no go on the Air

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.640
because he didn't have
the parts or information.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.820
Another independent
repair store in New York,

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.283
very similar pricing.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.150
So let's think about this for a second,

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:32.150
999 at Apple or 325 at Rossman Repair.

00:03:32.955 --> 00:03:35.070
I think it's pretty simple.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:36.650
An Apple spokesman said,

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:39.270
we believe the safest
and most reliable repair

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.260
is one handled by a trained technician

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.540
using Apple genuine parts,

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:45.980
and we continue to
expand Apple's offerings

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:48.310
to better meet our customer's needs.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:50.940
He added that Apple would
replace the entire motherboard

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.320
for a repair like this, which
makes it more expensive.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.072
But to really understand
how this legislation

00:03:56.072 --> 00:03:57.710
wants to change things,

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.620
I had to see what was happening
inside these machines.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:04.143
Here's the first one, it's
a 13 inch MacBook Pro.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.900
- The big deal is that
corrosion that is over here

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.540
is right next to where
the WiFi data line is.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.860
- Translation, the water cause
corrosion on the motherboard.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:17.300
And it's around components
that help with, oh, you know,

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:22.300
just three little things,
power, Wi-Fi and your screen,

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.820
but Rossman still thought it had a chance.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.010
- So let's see if this one
has a chance of working

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:28.710
because it doesn't look like
the corrosion around that area

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.220
is too bad.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:34.320
It is gonna, this is
like filing your nails.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.670
- That's not what filing
my nails looks like.

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:38.730
Now, two things really made this repair

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:39.890
possible for Rossman.

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:44.160
One, he had the extra parts to
swap in for the broken parts.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.070
He got them from what
are called donor boards,

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:50.970
boards from old systems
with still usable parts.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:54.130
Two, he had schematics and board views.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:56.670
These are basically maps
of what's in the system

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.150
so he can figure out what is what,

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:02.160
except these documents
created by the manufacturer

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.040
aren't provided to
independent repair shops.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.730
Rossman and others get these documents,

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:10.840
which are often leaked from
people that work at the company

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.070
from sites on the internet.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:16.070
That's part of what the right
to repair wants to change.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:17.910
So Apple doesn't give you any of these.

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.680
- No, and I can't get access to it

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.120
as a third party repair center.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.330
And if you become an
authorized repair center,

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:24.967
you still can't get access
to any of this stuff.

00:05:24.967 --> 00:05:26.782
- And was this originally
an Apple document?

00:05:26.782 --> 00:05:29.500
Is this like something Apple put together?

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.670
- Yeah, it says right in the bottom of it,

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:33.190
it says over here, notice
of proprietary property,

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:34.960
this information contained herein

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.090
is the proprietary property
of apple Apple Inc.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:38.780
- An Apple spokesperson
said that the company

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.090
has an independent
repair provider program,

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:43.660
which provides access
to Apple genuine parts,

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:46.710
tools, training, service
guides, diagnostics,

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.230
and resources to perform

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:50.700
a variety of out of warranty repairs.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:52.340
He added that the company has found

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.070
individual chip replacements
to be unreliable,

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:57.760
which is why it replaces
the full motherboard.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.370
However Rossman and others
say that this Apple program

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.079
does not provide schematics or board views

00:06:03.079 --> 00:06:04.750
and many specific parts

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:08.310
that make repairs more
affordable for consumers.

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.260
After about 15 minutes of work,

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.486
we were ready to see if it had been fixed.

00:06:13.486 --> 00:06:15.193
It's on.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:18.343
- It is on and it's charging.

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:20.800
- Okay, that was the MacBook Pro.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.750
But what about that MacBook Air?

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:23.653
- No.

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:29.250
- Rossman have access to
the board view document

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:33.430
for that specific MacBook Air,
which meant it was a no-go.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:35.780
- This one, even if I did
have all the information,

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:37.810
you know, it doesn't really
have the highest chance

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:38.730
of being repairable,

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:40.860
but when you combine the
factor that it looks like this

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.870
with the fact that I don't
have all the information

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.600
I need to do my job, I
would say, screw this.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:47.650
- Why don't you have the
information to do your job?

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:48.660
- Because I can't find the board view

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:49.670
because it's not available.

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.201
I have to wait for someone at Apple

00:06:51.201 --> 00:06:53.900
or one of these factories
to leak that board view

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:56.290
and risk their job or
risk them being in prison

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:58.500
to one of those websites
so that I can then buy it.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.090
- He also said that getting
the parts to fix the Air

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:03.003
aren't available yet to him.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:09.730
So to recap, this 13 inch
MacBook Pro works perfectly now.

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:12.930
I even wrote this script on it
and I've been working on it.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.002
This MacBook Air,

00:07:14.002 --> 00:07:16.860
well, my only choice is
really to go back to Apple

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.130
and pay them 799 to fix it,

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:22.520
or I can buy a completely
new MacBook Air for 999,

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.810
which really doesn't seem
like a choice at all.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.670
And that's what the right
to repair is all about.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.740
Giving us more choices
to repair our stuff.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:31.950
Tech companies and
opponents of the legislation

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:34.640
argue that that choice
can impact product safety

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:36.500
and data security and put companies'

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.620
intellectual property at risk.

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:41.674
What do you want to see the
most out of this legislation?

00:07:41.674 --> 00:07:44.200
- I'd like to see it have the teeth

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.220
to be able to do the bare minimum,

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:50.540
which is get us access to
schematics, to board views,

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:51.920
and to be able to buy these chips.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:53.420
It's odd to be a computer repairman

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:54.920
and feel like the kid in high school

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:56.930
that was selling weed
out of the fourth stall

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:57.870
of the lunch bathroom.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:01.030
- The future of all this now
rests in the hands of lawmakers

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:01.910
or in this case,

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:04.328
maybe it should be in the
hands of laptop makers

00:08:04.328 --> 00:08:06.883
who should consider liquid proofing.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:10.330
- You can tell when
cats hate their owners,

00:08:10.330 --> 00:08:12.930
based on the fact that they
piss on their computers.

